# TI-59/PC-100A Program: Calendar Printer, by Richard Vanderburgh
#
# From 52-Notes, V3N5p4. This is a series of calendar programs that started
# with this issue of 52-Notes. The issue contained three programs, and later
# editions kept adding faster and more compact programs. Our goal is to collect
# and preserve them here in the form of preload files.
#
# This is the documentation given for this program in the original article:
#
# Algorithm:
# 1. Check for ML module presence; alert user with flashing display if
#    absent.
# 2. Determine user's choice: 1 month, balance of start year, or print
#    from start month and year until end of specified year.
# 3. Call ML-20 SBR 086 with 1st day of specified month and year to get
#    corresponding absolute day, and ML-20 SBR 177 to get corresponding
#    day of week. Call ML-20 SBR 086 with 1st day of next month and
#    calculate difference between returned absolute day value, and first
#    one to get number of days in specified month.
# 4. Print month headers.
# 5. Use calculated day of week to initialize a general purpose print-
#    buffer-packer routine to print first line of days, followed by
#    successive lines until all days have been printed. Use a quasi base
#    seven counter to supply the print-buffer-packer routine with code
#    for successive integers.
# 6. For more months, as determined by step 2, cycle months, and increment
#    years as required; repeat steps 3-5 until done.
#
# User Instructions:
# 1. Initialize: Press A, see module confirmation printed; flashed
#    display indicates wrong module.
# 2. Key start year, press R/S; key start month, press R/S.
# 3. For 1 month: Press B;
#    For balance of year: Press C; for specified period: Key end year,
#    press D.
#
# Transcribed from a compact magazine/manual listing (OCR'd), same
# shorthand dialect as the other two presets in this series (see the
# calc-u-59-statefiles skill's "52-Notes" section), plus one convention
# not seen before in this series:
#   - A bare A-E/A'-E' key (with no GTO/SBR in front of it) is a direct,
#     unconditional 1-step jump to that label -- real TI-59 firmware
#     dispatches any of these ten keycodes to their Lbl unconditionally,
#     whether pressed interactively or hit as a stored program step. Seen
#     repeatedly here as "D' rtn", "C'", "A'", "B", "C" standing alone.
#   - Several branch instructions (GTO/SBR/Dsz/EQ/IFF) use the "arbitrary
#     keycode as a 1-byte label destination" trick already documented for
#     calendar-01's "SBR CE" case -- e.g. "SBR Write" is SBR with WRT's
#     own keycode (96) as the destination byte, "Dsz17 D'" targets D''s
#     keycode (19), "xXt E'" is EQ targeting E''s keycode (10).
# Program length reconstructed step-by-step and cross-checked against the
# step numbers printed at the start of every listing line (000, 028, 053,
# 080, 107, 133, 156, 189, 214, 244, 273, 301, 321, 351, 383, 414, 443) --
# every line's decoded length matches its declared next-line boundary
# exactly. The one OCR-noise fix needed: a stray leading "0" before "Op55"
# (~step 309) doesn't fit -- dropping it is what makes that line's length
# match.
# Uses Master Library program 20 (day-of-week / days-in-month helpers, via
# SBR 086 and SBR 177), hence SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML below.

MODEL: TI-59
SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML
PRINTER: on

CUECARD:
Template: MagnetCard
Title: Calendar Printer
Banks: 1,2
Row1: A, Year, R/S, Month, R/S, B (or C)
Row1Align: left
A: CHECK ML
B: 1 MONTH
C: TO YR END
D: END YEAR

